My updated hypothesis for the final SQL approach is:
Join the `superhero` table to the `gender` table on `superhero.gender_id = gender.id`, to the `publisher` table on `superhero.publisher_id = publisher.id`, and to the `colour` table three separate times (using aliases) on `superhero.eye_colour_id = colour.id`, `superhero.hair_colour_id = colour.id`, and `superhero.skin_colour_id = colour.id`. Filter by `gender.gender = 'Female'` and `publisher.publisher_name = 'Dark Horse Comics'`. Select the `colour` column from each of the three `colour` table aliases.

## PHASE 5 — CONFIRMATION QUERIES

1. -- RESOLVE [LIKE-VS-ENUM]: Check for any other variations of Dark Horse in publisher names.
   SELECT DISTINCT publisher_name FROM publisher WHERE publisher_name LIKE '%Dark Horse%' LIMIT 20;

